---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import GifTool from "../components/GifTool.astro";

const webAppSchema = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebApplication",
  "name": "Compress GIF Under 8MB",
  "url": Astro.url.href,
  "description": "Compress an animated GIF to exactly 8MB, or to any other target size, entirely in your browser. Also covers what the real platform limits are today — 8MB stopped being one in 2026.",
  "applicationCategory": "Multimedia",
  "operatingSystem": "Any",
  "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "0", "priceCurrency": "USD" },
  "featureList": ["Exact 8MB Target", "Any Custom Target Size", "Real-time Preview", "Client-side Processing"]
});
const faqSchema = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Is 8MB still a platform limit?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No. 8MB was Discord's free-tier cap for years, which is why the number is still everywhere, but Discord now accepts 20MB per attachment (raised from 10MB in August 2026) and X accepts GIFs up to 15MB. 8MB is still worth targeting when something else enforces it — older forum software, some CMS media libraries, legacy tools — or simply as a quality target that plays instantly almost anywhere." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "How do I compress a GIF to exactly 8MB?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Type 8 into the Any size box in the tool, leave the unit on MB, and press Go. The tool searches for the lightest compression level that still lands under 8MB, so you keep as much quality as the target allows. For GIFs originally over 30MB, resize to 600px width first — that alone often removes 75% of the bytes." } }
  ]
});
---
<Base
  title="Compress GIF Under 8MB — And Why That Limit Is Outdated"
  description="Compress an animated GIF to exactly 8MB or any target size, in your browser. Plus the current platform limits — 8MB is no longer one of them."
  schemas={[webAppSchema, faqSchema]}
>
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8 pb-6">
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">Compress GIF <span class="text-accent">Under 8MB</span></h1>
  <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mt-2 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
    Hit exactly 8MB — or any other target — in your browser, with no upload. If you are compressing for Discord, read the next section first: 8MB has not been its limit since 2026.
  </p>
</div>
<section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4"><GifTool /></section>

<article class="max-w-2xl mx-auto px-4 mt-16 pb-12">
  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Why everyone says 8MB — and why it is out of date</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">For years 8MB was Discord's free-tier upload cap, so "get your GIF under 8MB" became the standard advice and got copied into thousands of guides. Discord then raised that cap to 10MB and, in <strong class="text-slate-800">August 2026</strong>, to <strong class="text-slate-800">20MB</strong>. X (Twitter) accepts GIFs up to 15MB — the 5MB figure you see quoted is its <em>still image</em> limit, not its GIF limit.</p>
  <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong class="text-slate-800">So is 8MB still worth targeting?</strong> Yes, in two cases. First, when something other than a social platform enforces it: older forum software, some CMS media libraries, legacy tools, and plenty of internal systems still have a hard 8MB cap. Second, as a quality target in its own right — a GIF that size plays instantly almost anywhere, and it is a far more comfortable place to land than 20MB. If you are compressing for Discord specifically, <a href="/compress-gif-for-discord/" class="text-accent hover:underline">use the Discord guide instead</a>; you do not need to go this small.</p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">Current Platform Limits (all of them accept 8MB)</h2>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6"><table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
    <thead><tr class="border-b-2 border-slate-200 text-left"><th class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-slate-500 font-medium text-xs">Platform</th><th class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-slate-500 font-medium text-xs">Limit today</th><th class="py-2.5 text-slate-500 font-medium text-xs">Does 8MB pass?</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs font-medium text-slate-700">Discord (free)</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs">20MB <span class="text-slate-400">(was 8MB)</span></td><td class="py-2.5 text-xs text-accent font-medium">&check; With 12MB to spare</td></tr>
      <tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs font-medium text-slate-700">Discord (Nitro)</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs">500MB</td><td class="py-2.5 text-xs text-accent font-medium">&check; Easily</td></tr>
      <tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs font-medium text-slate-700">X / Twitter (GIF)</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs">15MB <span class="text-slate-400">(was 5MB)</span></td><td class="py-2.5 text-xs text-accent font-medium">&check; With 7MB to spare</td></tr>
      <tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs font-medium text-slate-700">WhatsApp</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs">16MB</td><td class="py-2.5 text-xs text-accent font-medium">&check; Well under</td></tr>
      <tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs font-medium text-slate-700">Facebook</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs">25MB</td><td class="py-2.5 text-xs text-accent font-medium">&check; Well under</td></tr>
      <tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs font-medium text-slate-700">Slack</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs">1GB</td><td class="py-2.5 text-xs text-accent font-medium">&check; Well under</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs font-medium text-slate-700">Reddit</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs">20MB</td><td class="py-2.5 text-xs text-accent font-medium">&check; Well under</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Every platform above accepts an 8MB GIF — which is exactly what makes 8MB a good target. It is low enough to clear every cap on this list plus the legacy 8MB systems that are not social platforms at all, and high enough to keep the quality most GIFs need.</p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Compress Any GIF Under 8MB</h2>
  <ol class="space-y-4 mb-6">
    <li class="text-sm text-slate-600"><strong class="text-slate-800 block mb-1">1. Drop your GIF into the tool above.</strong> Works with files up to 100MB. You can drag, paste, or click.</li>
    <li class="text-sm text-slate-600"><strong class="text-slate-800 block mb-1">2. Type 8 into the Any size box and press Go.</strong> The box sits next to the platform presets. The tool searches for the lightest level that still lands under 8MB, so you keep as much quality as the target allows.</li>
    <li class="text-sm text-slate-600"><strong class="text-slate-800 block mb-1">3. For very large files (30MB+), resize first.</strong> Use our <a href="/resize-gif/" class="text-accent hover:underline">GIF resizer</a> to reduce dimensions to 600px width. This alone can cut 75% off the file size before compression begins.</li>
    <li class="text-sm text-slate-600"><strong class="text-slate-800 block mb-1">4. Fine-tune with the compression slider.</strong> Balanced mode at level 40-60 works for most GIFs. For stubborn files, switch to Maximum mode at level 70-90.</li>
    <li class="text-sm text-slate-600"><strong class="text-slate-800 block mb-1">5. Download and share.</strong> Your compressed GIF is now compatible with nearly every social and messaging platform.</li>
  </ol>

  
  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step-by-Step: Compress Any GIF Under 8MB</h2>
  <ol class="space-y-4 mb-6">
    <li class="text-sm text-slate-600"><strong class="text-slate-800 block mb-1">1. Drop your GIF into the tool above.</strong> Drag and drop, paste from clipboard, or click to select. GIFs up to 100MB are accepted. Because processing happens in your browser, the file never leaves your device — no upload delay, no privacy risk.</li>
    <li class="text-sm text-slate-600"><strong class="text-slate-800 block mb-1">2. Type 8 into the Any size box and press Go.</strong> The tool calculates the compression level needed to bring your GIF under 8MB using whichever mode is selected. Balanced is a good starting point; switch to Max if 8MB is still out of reach.</li>
    <li class="text-sm text-slate-600"><strong class="text-slate-800 block mb-1">3. Fine-tune based on your GIF type.</strong> For memes and simple graphics, you can push to Maximum mode for even smaller files. For product demos and reaction GIFs with photos, stay in Balanced or switch to Quality to preserve details.</li>
    <li class="text-sm text-slate-600"><strong class="text-slate-800 block mb-1">4. Verify with the comparison slider.</strong> Drag the slider across your GIF at different points in the animation. Check for color banding in gradients, text readability, and frame smoothness. The A/B comparison catches artifacts that single-view previews miss.</li>
    <li class="text-sm text-slate-600"><strong class="text-slate-800 block mb-1">5. Download and test in your target app.</strong> Always test in the actual platform — Discord dark mode, Twitter feed, or your messaging app. Some platforms apply their own compression on top of yours, and dark backgrounds can make compression artifacts more visible.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">8MB vs Other Common Targets: A Comparison</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">If you are compressing to 8MB, you have likely considered other size targets. Here is how 8MB compares and when to choose it vs tighter alternatives:</p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6"><table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
    <thead><tr class="border-b-2 border-slate-200 text-left"><th class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-slate-500 font-medium text-xs">Target</th><th class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-slate-500 font-medium text-xs">Quality preservation</th><th class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-slate-500 font-medium text-xs">Platform coverage</th><th class="py-2.5 text-slate-500 font-medium text-xs">Best for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs font-medium text-slate-700">256KB</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs text-red-500 font-medium">Significant loss</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs">Every platform</td><td class="py-2.5 text-xs text-slate-500">Discord emoji, extreme CWV, email body embedded</td></tr>
      <tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs font-medium text-slate-700"><a href="/compress-gif-under-1mb/" class="text-accent hover:underline">1MB</a></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs text-amber-600 font-medium">Moderate loss</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs">Every platform</td><td class="py-2.5 text-xs text-slate-500">Email campaigns, web embeds, fast-loading social</td></tr>
      <tr class="border-b border-slate-100 bg-accent/[0.02]"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs font-medium text-slate-700"><strong class="text-accent">8MB</strong></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs text-accent font-medium"><strong class="text-accent">Minimal to moderate loss</strong></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs">Every platform, plus legacy 8MB systems</td><td class="py-2.5 text-xs text-slate-500"><strong class="text-slate-700">Universal social sharing with good quality</strong></td></tr>
      <tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs font-medium text-slate-700">16MB</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs text-accent font-medium">Good</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs">WhatsApp, Telegram, Slack</td><td class="py-2.5 text-xs text-slate-500">High-quality sharing where the platform allows it</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs font-medium text-slate-700">20MB</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs text-accent font-medium">Excellent</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 text-xs">Discord free, Reddit, Facebook</td><td class="py-2.5 text-xs text-slate-500">The largest a free Discord upload can be</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">8MB is the sweet spot for sharing. It preserves enough quality for most GIFs to look good, it clears every platform limit in the table above, and it also clears the legacy 8MB caps that still exist outside social media. Below 8MB you are making increasingly painful quality trade-offs; above it you gain quality but lose compatibility with those older systems. Unless you have a specific reason to go larger, 8MB is the safest single number to aim at.</p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">When 8MB Is Enough — and When It Is Not</h2>
  <div class="space-y-3 mb-6">
    <div class="tool-card p-4"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900">&check; 8MB is perfect for:</h4><p class="text-xs text-slate-500 mt-1">Reaction GIFs and memes shared on Discord and Twitter. Product demos and tutorials shared with colleagues on Slack. High-quality animations shared on Facebook and WhatsApp. Most GIFs under 30 seconds, at reasonable dimensions (600-800px wide), easily compress to 2-5MB in Balanced mode — giving you a comfortable margin below the limit.</p></div>
    <div class="tool-card p-4"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900">&times; 8MB is not enough for:</h4><p class="text-xs text-slate-500 mt-1">Long screen recordings (30+ seconds). High-resolution cinematic GIFs (1920px+). GIFs with hundreds of frames at 30fps. Uncompressed source files exported directly from video editors. For these cases, <a href="/gif-to-webp/" class="text-accent hover:underline">WebP</a> or <a href="/gif-to-mp4/" class="text-accent hover:underline">MP4</a> conversion will produce far better results than trying to squeeze them into 8MB as GIF.</p></div>
    <div class="tool-card p-4"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900">Pro tip: aim for 2-5MB, not exactly 8MB</h4><p class="text-xs text-slate-500 mt-1">An 8MB target guarantees compatibility, but for the best experience do not spend the whole budget. A 2-5MB GIF loads instantly in chat as an inline preview; a 7.8MB one may display as a download link or take several seconds to buffer. Set the Any size box to 5 MB instead of 8 MB and you get both.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">Batch Processing for 8MB Targets</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">If you manage a meme library, social media content calendar, or asset pipeline, compressing to 8MB one at a time is slow. Use Batch mode in the tool above:</p>
  <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1 mb-4 text-sm text-slate-600">
    <li>Toggle <strong class="text-slate-800">Batch mode</strong> in the GifTool component</li>
    <li>Drop or select multiple GIFs — up to 20 at once</li>
    <li>Set the target to 8 MB before compressing — every file in the batch uses the same settings</li>
    <li>All compressed GIFs download as a single ZIP</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Tip: For mixed batches (some memes, some photos, some screen recordings), use Balanced mode at level 50. Quality mode may leave some files above 8MB, and Maximum mode may destroy the photographic ones. Balanced 50 splits the difference and gives consistent results across content types.</p>
<div class="mt-8 pt-6 border-t border-slate-100">
    <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">Related: <a href="/compress-gif-under-1mb/" class="text-accent hover:underline">Compress Under 1MB</a> &middot; <a href="/compress-gif-to-256kb/" class="text-accent hover:underline">Compress to 256KB</a> &middot; <a href="/compress-gif-for-discord/" class="text-accent hover:underline">Compress for Discord</a> &middot; <a href="/reduce-gif-size/" class="text-accent hover:underline">Platform Size Guide</a></p>
  </div>
</article>
</Base>
